# HyperMarkDown Tutorial

Your reading club keeps a note for each book. Before the next meeting, you
want to share a passage from one of those notes and see which books the club
still has to read. In this tutorial, you'll put both on one page. The passage
comes from a book card, and a query builds the reading list from the cards'
reading status.

You can follow along with a text editor. Install `hmd` to check and render the
cards, and `hql` to run the query. [Quick start](../tools/index.md)

## Write a book card

Create a vault for the club and a folder for the books.

```sh
hmd init reading-club
cd reading-club
mkdir books
```

`hmd init` creates `.hmd/config.toml` and makes `reading-club/` a **vault**:
the directory where the club's cards are stored. Like `.git/` in a Git
repository, `.hmd/` holds technical data about the vault. Today that is the
configuration, and HQL and its extensions can store their own data there, such
as a search index.
In this example the vault directory is also the **namespace root**, the base
for card paths such as `books/emma.hmd`.

Save the first book card as `books/emma.hmd`.

```hmd
---
title: Emma
metadata:
  author: Jane Austen
  status: todo
---

# Emma

## Discussion

Emma is quick to see what others want and slow to see what she wants. ^discussion
```

A **card** is a document you can refer to from another document. This `.hmd`
card has two parts. The Markdown body holds the club's notes. The optional
YAML block at the top, called **frontmatter**, records facts that tools can
read by name. Later, the query will look for `todo` under `metadata.status`.

The `^discussion` at the end of the paragraph is a **block anchor**. It gives
that paragraph an identifier and disappears when the card is rendered.

Save the second card as `books/dune.hmd`.

```hmd
---
title: Dune
metadata:
  author: Frank Herbert
  status: read
---

# Dune

Who controls the spice, and what does it cost them to keep control?
```

A vault can also contain `.md` and `.markdown` cards. You can link to them
and embed them. Use `.hmd` when the document itself uses HyperMarkDown syntax.

## Link to the discussion

Create `next.hmd` at the root of the vault.

```hmd
# Next meeting

Start with [[books/emma|Emma]] and open the [[books/emma#Discussion|discussion notes]].
```

A **wikilink** uses `[[` and `]]`. Here, `books/emma` selects `books/emma.hmd`
and `#Discussion` selects a heading in that card. The text after `|` is what
the reader sees. You can also point to the paragraph by its block anchor with
`[[books/emma#^discussion]]`. That reference still identifies the same
paragraph if you rename the heading above it.

With the default settings, `[[emma]]` would also work here. The resolver
checks the current directory and its parents, then searches the namespace if
it has found nothing. If you later add `archive/emma.hmd`, that last search
finds two cards and reports an ambiguity. `[[/books/emma]]` selects the book
from the namespace root directly. Imports and the full search order are
covered in [[/public/namespaces|Namespaces]].

## Reuse a passage

To put the discussion prompt on the meeting page, add an embed below the
links in `next.hmd`.

```hmd
### Discussion prompt

![[books/emma#^discussion]]
```

Run `hmd render next.hmd` from the vault directory. The output includes this
paragraph under **Discussion prompt**.

> Emma is quick to see what others want and slow to see what she wants.

The `!` makes the reference an **embed**. The rendered meeting page contains
the paragraph, and the book card still holds the text you edit. You can also
embed a section with `![[books/emma#Discussion]]` or the whole body with
`![[books/emma]]`. Links in an embedded passage still resolve from the card
where they were written.

## Generate the reading list

The reading list comes from the book cards themselves. Add a query to
`next.hmd` that looks for books whose status is `todo`.

````hmd
## Still to read

```hql#eval
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.status == "todo")
| sort(by = c => c.title)
```
````

`cards` is the collection of cards in the vault. The first step keeps the cards
whose status is `todo`. The second sorts them by title. The result is a list of
cards, so HQL writes a list of wikilinks. The query reads the metadata on the
same cards the club uses for its notes.

`hql#eval` marks a cell whose result can be saved in the document. Run it from
the vault directory.

```sh
hql render --in-place --emit after next.hmd
```

Open `next.hmd` again. HQL adds the following line below the query's closing
fence, between two HTML comments.

```hmd
- [[emma]]
```

Run `hmd render next.hmd` again and the list item becomes a link to the book
card. Saved results are part of the document, so readers can follow that link
without running HQL. To see where the list came from, read the cell above it.

Now change Emma's status to `read` and Dune's to `todo`. Run the same HQL command.
The saved list now contains `[[dune]]`. The discussion prompt still comes from
*Emma*, the book you chose for the meeting. You can change which passage to
discuss by editing the embed. To update the reading list, edit a book card
and rerun the query.

Use a plain `hql` fence to show a query as code. Use `hql#query id=books` for a
cell whose value other cells can read as `books` without adding its own output
to the page. The [example notebook](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/tree/main/examples/notebook)
uses a shared value to build a contents list and count the books still to read.

## Check the vault

```sh
hmd lint
```

Try adding `[[meeting-minutes]]` to `next.hmd` and run the check again. The
linter reports that the target is missing and tells you where you wrote the
link. You can leave it as a reminder or add `meeting-minutes.hmd` to resolve
it. A missing target is a warning. An ambiguous name, such as the two copies
of `emma` above, is an error and needs a more specific reference.

The linter also checks anchors, frontmatter and embeds. If you want to write a
wikilink as an example, put it in a code span or a fenced code block.

## Publish the meeting page

A local card is private by default. To publish the meeting page and the book
cards with the HyperMarkDown site plugin, add a folder note at `index.hmd`.

```hmd
---
nav:
  visibility: public
---

# Reading club

See [[next|the next meeting]].
```

Cards under this folder inherit `public` unless they set their own
`nav.visibility`. This makes the whole example vault public. For a vault with
private notes, set `nav.visibility: public` on the individual cards you want
to publish. A public page can only link to or embed a card on the site if its
target is published too. See the [site setup](../public/presentation.hmd) for
building and viewing the site.

## Further reading

- [[/public/vision|Vision]] explains how cards can describe a system, compute
  over it, and keep the results available for the next revision.
- [[/public/features|Features]] shows the format's callouts, math, and diagrams.
- [[hmd-lang-spec|Language Specification]] defines the syntax, including
  frontmatter, reference resolution, and cell kinds.
- [Cell output](../public/cell-output.hmd) shows what HQL writes for each kind of
  result. [Queries inside documents](https://hql-lang.org/latest/book/24-documents/)
  explains running cells and sharing values between them.
